---
import { Badge } from '@/components/ui/badge';
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/hakula139/assistant-bench';
const docs = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/design`;

const sections = {
  rounds: 'How a round works',
  scores: 'Scores and intervals',
  leaderboard: 'Reading the leaderboard',
  leader: 'How the leader is chosen',
  summary: 'Round summary',
  tests: 'Boards scored by tests',
  limits: 'Known limits',
} as const;

const columns = [
  {
    term: 'Rank',
    definition:
      'The best to the worst rank the intervals allow: 1 plus the systems whose interval lies wholly above this one, to 1 plus those whose interval reaches above its lower end.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Preliminary',
    badge: true,
    definition:
      'Fewer than 40 decisive votes, meaning votes won by either side, so the score may still move. The plot draws it as a hollow dot.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Unranked',
    definition:
      'Listed below the table without a score until enough comparisons connect the system to the reference.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Votes',
    definition:
      "The rater's votes on the system. Results settled without a vote count toward the score but not here.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Both bad',
    definition: 'The share of those votes that judged both outputs bad.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Gate failures',
    definition: "The share of the system's outputs that failed a gate.",
  },
  {
    term: 'Cost / output',
    definition: 'The mean cost of one output at list prices.',
  },
  {
    term: 'p50 latency',
    definition: 'The median time from the request to the complete response.',
  },
];

const diagnostics = [
  {
    term: 'Rater agreement',
    definition:
      'How often a side-swapped repeat matched the original vote. Below 70%, the intervals overstate how well systems separate.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Separated pairs',
    definition: 'The share of system pairs whose intervals do not overlap.',
  },
  {
    term: 'Split-half τ',
    definition:
      "Kendall's τ between the orders fit to odd and to even sessions, where 1 means both halves rank the systems the same.",
  },
];

const h2 = 'scroll-mt-6 text-lg font-semibold target:text-link';
const dl = 'grid gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr] sm:gap-x-6 sm:gap-y-2';
const list = 'space-y-2 pl-6 marker:text-muted-foreground';
---

<Base title="Methodology">
  <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,42rem)_13rem] lg:justify-between lg:gap-x-16 lg:gap-y-10">
    <header class="space-y-3 lg:col-start-1">
      <h1 class="text-[1.75rem] leading-tight font-semibold">Methodology</h1>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Every system in a family answers the same cases, and one rater compares the outputs two at a
        time without seeing which system wrote which.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav
      aria-label="On this page"
      class="text-sm lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:col-start-2 lg:row-span-2 lg:row-start-1 lg:self-start"
    >
      <p class="mb-2 font-medium">On this page</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1.5">
        {Object.entries(sections).map(([id, title]) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${id}`} class="text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground">
              {title}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="space-y-10 leading-relaxed lg:col-start-1">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="rounds" class={h2}>
          {sections.rounds}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            A family is one kind of task with its own cases and leaderboard. Every system in it, a
            model with its effort, harness, and prompt, answers the same cases with the same
            instruction, and changing any part makes a new system.
          </li>
          <li>
            Gates check each output for problems such as truncation or the wrong script. A failing
            output loses without a vote, and two identical outputs tie.
          </li>
          <li>
            The rater sees two outputs side by side without system names and picks the better one, a
            tie, or both bad, in sessions of up to 30 pairs. Sides are counterbalanced, and some
            pairs return later swapped to check the rater's consistency.
          </li>
          <li>
            Each round is a dated snapshot that refits every vote cast so far, so its board is
            cumulative.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="scores" class={h2}>
          {sections.scores}
        </h2>
        <p>
          A Bradley-Terry model turns the votes and the results settled without a vote into scores
          on an Elo-like scale, with the family's reference system at 1,000 and ties and both-bad
          votes counting as half a win. A 100-point gap means the higher system is expected to win
          about 64% of the votes between them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 95% intervals come from 1,000 bootstrap resamples of whole cases, since votes on one
          case are correlated. Each category's board refits that category's votes alone, so its
          intervals are wider and it shows no rank.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leaderboard" class={h2}>
          {sections.leaderboard}
        </h2>
        <p>
          The plot draws each 95% interval on one shared axis, with a vertical line at the reference
          system's score.
        </p>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {columns.map(({ term, badge, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">
                {badge ? (
                  <Badge variant="outline" className="font-normal text-muted-foreground">
                    {term}
                  </Badge>
                ) : (
                  term
                )}
              </dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          A dash marks a missing value. In a category view, the four columns marked all categories
          still cover the whole family.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="leader" class={h2}>
          {sections.leader}
        </h2>
        <ol class:list={['list-decimal', list]}>
          <li>
            Every ranked system whose score difference from the top one has a 95% interval
            containing 0, or an unbounded one, ties for the top. The difference is paired within
            each resample, so two systems can separate even when their intervals overlap.
          </li>
          <li>
            While any system tied for the top, the top one included, is preliminary, the round names
            no leader.
          </li>
          <li>
            Otherwise a system alone at the top leads, and a tie goes to the lower cost per output,
            then the lower median latency, then the higher score, with a known value beating a
            missing one.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="summary" class={h2}>
          {sections.summary}
        </h2>
        <p>The summary above each board reports how far its order can be trusted.</p>
        <dl class={dl}>
          {diagnostics.map(({ term, definition }) => (
            <div class="sm:contents">
              <dt class="font-medium">{term}</dt>
              <dd>{definition}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p>
          A vote leaves the fit, though it stays on record, when its case is retired, when either
          output was removed or now fails a gate, or when the two outputs now settle the pair
          without a vote.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="tests" class={h2}>
          {sections.tests}
        </h2>
        <p>
          Some families run each system on tasks that automated tests check, where each run is a
          trial. Pass rate is the share of trials that pass, with errored trials counted as failures
          and tallied under Errored. Cost / trial and p50 latency are a trial's mean cost and median
          time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 id="limits" class={h2}>
          {sections.limits}
        </h2>
        <ul class:list={['list-disc', list]}>
          <li>One person casts every vote, so each board reflects that person's preferences.</li>
          <li>
            Scores are relative, so the both-bad and gate-failure rates are the only absolute
            signals.
          </li>
          <li>Each family anchors its own reference at 1,000, so scores compare only within it.</li>
          <li>Votes are few, so only clear preferences separate.</li>
          <li>
            Scores carry no length adjustment. Each system page reports whether the rater favors
            longer or shorter outputs.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground">
          The design notes on <a href={`${docs}/ranking.md`}>ranking</a>, the{' '}
          <a href={`${docs}/arena.md`}>vote arena</a>, and each{' '}
          <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/docs/design/families`}>family</a> cover the math.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
